---
// Built from Max's canvas frame 2793:3161, which he approved on 2026-08-23.
// Sections in canvas order, on the site's 160px rhythm — verified against the
// canvas rather than assumed: every gap between sections there is exactly 160
// (Hero ends 900 / Lead 1060, Lead ends 1483 / Industries 1643, and so on).
//
// This is the feat-074 port, and it is a rebuild rather than a patch. What
// changed against the version built from 2690:10957:
//   * Industries is a five-column segmented bar, not a donut.
//   * Availability is a Budapest -> open-to-relocate arrow block.
//   * The portrait + 4-card link row is GONE — the canvas dropped it.
//   * Two 1000px media rows arrived below Hobbies.
//   * Contact leads with mail, and the email lives inside that card.
//     (Superseded by feat-099 — the cards are gone; Contact is one button.)
//
// Chase 2793:3161 and nothing older. Max works by duplicating the page, so
// 2778:1840 and 2690:10957 are both gone or parked; the node ids in the old
// comments here pointed at frames that no longer exist.
//
// The hero and all six slots below Hobbies now carry real assets (feat-095).
// They were placeholders because Max hadn't supplied files yet, not because
// the design was unsettled — see the slot table above mediaRows for which
// Figma node each file came from.
//
// `public/media/about/portrait.jpg` is STILL deliberately unreferenced, and no
// longer a candidate for anything: it's the cat photo, and the canvas has no
// slot holding it. Kept on disk under Max's "don't remove images" rule.
//
// `public/media/about/desk.jpg` joined it as of feat-097: Max re-pointed the
// row-2 stack-bottom slot (2799:3371) at a party photo, so the tight
// 791.5x468 desk crop has no slot on this page any more. It stays on disk
// under the same rule, NOT because anything still reads it.
//
// The desk SCENE is still on the site — as a wider 1647x800 crop on /process,
// from a different node (`2712:2273`). That is a separate export, not this
// file reused, and it does not live in this folder.
import Layout from '@app/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Button, Heading, HungaryFlag, CtaCard } from '@shared/ui';
import { Media } from '@entities/media';
import { PageHeader } from '@widgets/page-header';
import { ProjectHero } from '@widgets/project-hero';
import { OverviewSection } from '@widgets/overview-section';
import { IndustriesBar } from '@widgets/industries-bar';

const EMAIL = 'max.shturma@gmail.com';

// Canvas 2793:3224 (feat-099): Contact is now ONE accent button carrying the
// email address, not the two stat-cards it shipped with. The section went
// 588px -> 180px on canvas: 56 statement + 64 gap + 60 button.
//
// The Download CV card is GONE, and that is not a lost route — feat-082 put CV
// in the sidebar on every page, which is what freed Contact to do one thing.
// The mail-first ordering this replaces (which itself reversed bug-051) is now
// moot: there is nothing left to order.
//
// The button's glyph is `mail`, and the canvas will not tell you that by name.
// Its icon layer is called `right-arrow` because that is the Button master's
// slot name; the exported path is an envelope, byte-identical to this
// project's `mail.svg`. Read the geometry, not the layer name.

// The arrowhead from canvas 2794:3323, exported bytes verbatim.
//
// The canvas arrow is one 506x0 stroked vector with an arrow end-cap, which
// exports as a single filled path: a 4px shaft (the rect at y 26-30) plus the
// chevron. It cannot ship as one stretched SVG — the export carries
// preserveAspectRatio="none", so widening it to fill a fluid column would
// smear the head horizontally.
//
// So the shaft is CSS (a plain 4px block; there is no shape in a straight line
// to get wrong) and this SVG carries the head. Its viewBox starts at x=480
// rather than at the head's own left edge, which keeps 26px of the exported
// shaft attached as a stub — that is what makes the join to the CSS shaft
// seamless instead of a butt-joint with a visible notch, since both are the
// same 4px band centred on y=28.
const ARROW_VIEWBOX = '480 13.0768 28.195 29.8464';
const ARROW_PATH =
  'M507.414 29.4142C508.195 28.6332 508.195 27.3668 507.414 26.5858L494.686 13.8579C493.905 13.0768 492.639 13.0768 491.858 13.8579C491.077 14.6389 491.077 15.9052 491.858 16.6863L503.172 28L491.858 39.3137C491.077 40.0948 491.077 41.3611 491.858 42.1421C492.639 42.9232 493.905 42.9232 494.686 42.1421L507.414 29.4142ZM0 28V30H506V28V26H0V28Z';

// Canvas 2799:3373. Two 1000px rows, 64px apart, each a 2-column split with a
// 64px gutter; the big slot swaps sides between rows. Slot heights are 468 +
// 64 + 468 = 1000, which is why the stacked pair ends level with the tall one.
// `tallFirst` is which side the full-height slot takes in that row.
//
// Which file goes where is NOT a guess. Max named two of the slot frames after
// the files themselves, and the videos' pixel dimensions match those exact
// slots, so the two constraints agree:
//
//   row  slot            Figma node    box         file
//   1    tall (left)     2793:3220     791.5x1000  dt-lecture.webm   (792x1000)
//   1    stack top       2793:3222     791.5x468   photography.jpg
//   1    stack bottom    2793:3223     791.5x468   workshop.jpg
//   2    stack top       2799:3370     791.5x468   wireframing.webm  (792x468)
//   2    stack bottom    2799:3371     791.5x468   celebration.jpg
//   2    tall (right)    2799:3368     791.5x1000  team.jpg
//
// Frames 2793:3220 and 2799:3370 are literally named `about-dt-lecture.webm`
// and `about-wireframing.webm` on canvas. The four stills are 2x renders of
// their slot FRAME rather than the raw fill, which is what preserves Figma's
// own crop — 2793:3222 is a STRETCH fill with a crop transform, and 2793:3223
// is a background fill with a 791x519 photo layered over it at y-25. Pulling
// raw fills would have lost both.
//
// Chase 2793:3161 for all of these. The loose `About` frame 2851:5316 is a
// stale duplicate: same 1920x5767 box, but its slots are all still named
// `image` and it carries the retired Budapest crest.
type AboutMedia =
  | { kind: 'image'; src: string; alt: string }
  | { kind: 'video'; src: string; poster: string; alt: string };

interface AboutMediaRow {
  tallFirst: boolean;
  tall: AboutMedia;
  stack: [AboutMedia, AboutMedia];
}

const mediaRows: AboutMediaRow[] = [
  {
    tallFirst: true,
    tall: {
      kind: 'video',
      src: '/media/about/dt-lecture.webm',
      poster: '/media/about/dt-lecture-poster.jpg',
      alt: 'Max presenting a Design Thinking session to a workshop group',
    },
    stack: [
      {
        kind: 'image',
        src: '/media/about/photography.jpg',
        alt: 'Max standing on a mountain ridge overlooking jagged peaks',
      },
      {
        kind: 'image',
        src: '/media/about/workshop.jpg',
        alt: 'A workshop group posing in front of the session screen',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    tallFirst: false,
    tall: {
      kind: 'image',
      src: '/media/about/team.jpg',
      alt: 'Max with his team at a karting track',
    },
    stack: [
      {
        kind: 'video',
        src: '/media/about/wireframing.webm',
        poster: '/media/about/wireframing-poster.jpg',
        alt: 'Sketching paper wireframes and drawing gothic lettering by hand',
      },
      {
        kind: 'image',
        src: '/media/about/celebration.jpg',
        alt: 'Max and a guest at an evening party, pink balloons and flower leis behind them',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="About — Max Shturma">
  <main class="about">
    {
      /* PageHeader's title is a <span> that's display:none below 1024px, so
        without this the page has no h1 at any width (feat-047's finding). */
    }
    <h1 class="visually-hidden">About</h1>

    <PageHeader title="About" class="about__header" />

    {
      /* Canvas 2793:3169 — a 1647x800 crop of Max's portrait. Passing `media`
        retires ProjectHero's --placeholder branch with no other change, which
        is exactly what that component's own comment says it's there for.

        This node is still current and still the beanie portrait — feat-097
        briefly replaced it with a desk crop and that was a MISREAD, corrected
        the same day. The desk crop belongs to `2712:2273`, which is the image
        instance inside `2712:2272`, the /process hero. Both are
        `project-hero-image` instances at 1647x800, so the node dimensions
        alone do not tell them apart; the parent does. Walk up before porting
        a hero. */
    }
    <ProjectHero
      media={{
        type: 'image',
        image: '/media/about/hero.jpg',
        imageAlt: 'Max Shturma in a green beanie against a rust-coloured backdrop',
      }}
      scrollCue
    />

    {
      /* Lead copy is copy-044 (Figma node 2793:3176's Lead text), rewritten
        2026-09-15 to lead with Product Designer per .context/identity.md's
        repositioned priority order — replaces copy-040, which led with
        Design Engineer for the same reason under the superseded 2026-08-17
        ordering. The "nine years, last one in code" structure is unchanged;
        only the title and the middle clause moved. Also drops "seamless", which
        .context/identity.md's own voice rules ban as buzzword fluff. Skills
        list is copy-040 too — Figma node 3183:2633 — reordered to lead with
        the two design-engineering bullets (code stack + AI-assisted
        workflow), merging the old "UI Kit & Design System creation" bullet
        into the first (now code-based) and the old "Prototyping (various
        fidelity)" into the client-presentation one, keeping 9 items total.
        The eight-pill tag row that used to sit under the skills list is gone
        on Max's call. */
    }
    <OverviewSection
      heading="Since 2017"
      class="about__section"
      list={[
        'Scalable design systems (tokens, components, documentation) in React, TypeScript & Astro',
        'AI-assisted design-to-code workflow with Claude Code (Figma to production)',
        'UX research (interviews, competitor analysis, user testing)',
        'A/B testing for improvement & conversion',
        'Product/website redesign & complex UX problem solving',
        'Color, typography & composition',
        'Prototyping (wireframe to high-fidelity) for client presentation',
        'Landing page development (client-specific)',
        'Multi-platform design (desktop, mobile)',
      ]}
    >
      I'm Max Shturma, a product designer. Nine years of UX/UI design, and the last one spent
      building in code. I design products end to end — research, flows, design systems — and ship
      them myself in React, TypeScript, and Astro, from Figma to production with Claude Code.
    </OverviewSection>

    <section class="about__section section-grid">
      <Heading class="section-grid__aside">Industries</Heading>
      <div class="section-grid__body" data-reveal>
        <IndustriesBar />
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      /* Canvas 2793:3213: a mark over "Budapest", a long arrow, then the date
        over "Open to relocate" — right-aligned, so the two city/status lines
        bracket the arrow and the date hangs above the second one. The row is
        align-items: end because the arrow has to meet the two 40px baselines,
        not the top of the mark. Canvas draws that mark as the Budapest coat of
        arms; code ships the flag of Hungary instead (feat-094) because the
        municipal arms is a permit-gated symbol and the flag is not. A known,
        deliberate divergence from the canvas — see HungaryFlag.astro. */
    }
    <section class="about__section section-grid">
      <Heading class="section-grid__aside">Availability</Heading>
      <div class="section-grid__body about__availability" data-reveal>
        {
          /* The row is a child so the body above it can be the query
            container — an element cannot container-query itself, and
            `flex-direction` has to change on the row. Same root-plus-inner-row
            shape industries-bar uses, for the same reason. */
        }
        <div class="about__availability-row">
          <div class="about__place">
            <HungaryFlag />
            <p class="about__place-name">Budapest</p>
          </div>

          <div class="about__arrow" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="about__arrow-shaft"></span>
            <svg
              class="about__arrow-head"
              viewBox={ARROW_VIEWBOX}
              fill="none"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              focusable="false"
            >
              <path d={ARROW_PATH} fill="currentColor"></path>
            </svg>
          </div>

          <div class="about__destination">
            <p class="about__from">From January 2027</p>
            <p class="about__place-name">Open to relocate</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      /* No `aria-hidden` any more. These six are a real photo/video set about
        Max, not decoration standing in for absent art, so every slot carries
        alt text instead of being hidden from assistive tech wholesale.

        Videos go through <Media> rather than a bare <video> so they inherit
        its IntersectionObserver autoplay (paused off-screen, and off entirely
        under prefers-reduced-motion or below tablet), its .mp4 fallback
        derived from the .webm basename, and its poster. */
    }
    <section class="about__section about__media">
      {
        mediaRows.map((row) => (
          <div
            class:list={['about__media-row', { 'about__media-row--reversed': !row.tallFirst }]}
            data-reveal
          >
            <div class="about__media-tall">
              {row.tall.kind === 'video' ? (
                <Media
                  type="video"
                  src={row.tall.src}
                  poster={row.tall.poster}
                  alt={row.tall.alt}
                  class="about__media-asset"
                />
              ) : (
                <img
                  class="about__media-asset"
                  src={row.tall.src}
                  alt={row.tall.alt}
                  loading="lazy"
                  data-media-skeleton
                />
              )}
            </div>
            <div class="about__media-stack">
              {row.stack.map((slot) => (
                <div class="about__media-slot">
                  {slot.kind === 'video' ? (
                    <Media
                      type="video"
                      src={slot.src}
                      poster={slot.poster}
                      alt={slot.alt}
                      class="about__media-asset"
                    />
                  ) : (
                    <img
                      class="about__media-asset"
                      src={slot.src}
                      alt={slot.alt}
                      loading="lazy"
                      data-media-skeleton
                    />
                  )}
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </section>

    <section class="about__section section-grid">
      <Heading class="section-grid__aside">Contact</Heading>
      <div class="section-grid__body about__contact" data-reveal>
        <p class="section-prose">If any of this fits what you're hiring for, get in touch.</p>
        <Button type="solid" href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`} icon="mail" class="about__contact-button">
          {EMAIL}
        </Button>
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      /* CTA card, Figma node 2964:2287 (copy-027). Replaces the old plain
        ButtonText link that used to render inside OverviewSection's `link`
        prop — that prop is now dead everywhere and removed from the widget.
        Its own section-grid row, aside "System", matching the page's own
        PageHeader title on /system rather than the mockup's first-draft
        "Portfolio" label. Markup lives in the shared CtaCard component
        (feat-112) once /system's Repository card became a second consumer.

        Placed last, after Contact — Figma's own "Portfolio System" frame
        sits at the very end of the canvas (right before the back-to-top
        corner), not right after the Lead section where it first shipped.

        Heading reworded from "My design system" (Max's review): read as a
        generic, portable personal design system rather than one specific to
        this site. Background swapped from a placeholder forest photo to
        Figma's actual fill (node 2964:2311) — a code/tokens texture, dark
        enough on its own (avg ~18/255) that Figma's own composition never
        adds a scrim layer on top of it either; `scrim={false}` here for the
        same reason the Repository card drops it. Even Figma's own darkness
        read as too dark once Max saw it live, so the asset itself was
        brightened ~1.9x (PIL, same crop/content) on top of dropping the
        scrim — a deliberate deviation from the exact Figma export, not a
        mismatch.

        MUST be exported as the plain image FILL only (the raw asset URL
        get_design_context returns for the node), never a get_screenshot of
        the whole frame — that flattens Figma's OWN heading/caption text
        layers into the image too, which then visually doubles against this
        component's own live heading/caption on top of it. Cost a real bug
        once already (Max caught it live): the flattened-frame export read
        fine at low brightness (baked text invisible against near-black) and
        only became visible as ghosting once the image was brightened. */
    }
    <section class="about__section section-grid">
      <Heading class="section-grid__aside">System</Heading>
      <div class="section-grid__body" data-reveal>
        <CtaCard
          href="/system"
          image="/media/about/system-cta.jpg"
          imageAlt=""
          heading="This portfolio's design system"
          caption="Tokens and components, live from the code."
          scrim={false}
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      /* No page-local back-to-top. The canvas shows one in this corner, but
        `features/scroll-to-top` is already global chrome mounted once in
        Layout.astro — a viewport-fixed button that appears after a screen of
        scrolling — and its own comment records that exact decision for this
        Figma node (2377:2011). feat-066 added a second one here and it was a
        straight duplicate. */
    }
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* :global() — the class merges onto PageHeader's own rendered element, which
     carries PageHeader.astro's astro-cid, not this page's. */
  :global(.about__header) {
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  .about {
    padding-bottom: 160px;
  }

  /* The site's 160px section rhythm, same schedule process.astro uses.
     :global() for the same reason as .about__header above, and this one was a
     real bug: `about__section` is passed as a prop to <OverviewSection>, whose
     root element carries OverviewSection's astro-cid rather than this page's,
     so a scoped rule never matched it. That one miss produced four visible
     defects at once — the overview bled to the viewport edge (padding-left 0
     against every other section's 40px), its aside label sat 40px left of the
     other four, its body column started 13px left of theirs and ran 53px
     wider, and the hero-to-overview gap was 0px where every other gap was
     exactly 160px. The plain <section> elements below matched fine either way,
     which is what made it look like an alignment problem rather than a
     scoping one.

     ⚠ The generalisable rule, and the reason this comment is this long: any
     class handed to a child component as a prop must be :global(). */
  :global(.about__section) {
    padding: 0 40px;
    margin-top: 160px;
  }

  /* --- Availability (canvas 2793:3213) --- */

  .about__availability {
    container: availability / inline-size;
  }
  .about__availability-row {
    display: flex;
    /* Canvas gap between the three parts. */
    gap: 40px;
    /* end, not center: the flag sits ABOVE "Budapest" and the date ABOVE
       "Open to relocate", so the only line the three parts share is the
       bottom one, which is where the arrow belongs. */
    align-items: flex-end;
  }

  .about__place,
  .about__destination {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: none;
  }
  /* flex-start, not the default stretch (bug-058, kept through feat-094). The
     original defect was the traced crest sitting 45.8px right of the word: as
     a stretched column item its inline-flex span filled the column's full
     177.6px and centred the artwork inside itself. That component is gone and
     HungaryFlag has no internal centring to defeat, so the rule no longer
     fixes a bug — but it is still what pins the flag's left edge to the word's
     at x=0, which is where canvas puts them. Stretch on a column container
     stretches the cross axis, so dropping this would leave the flag's box
     free to grow with the column again.
     Scoped to .about__place on purpose: .about__destination right-aligns and
     sets its own `align-items: flex-end` below. */
  .about__place {
    align-items: flex-start;
  }
  .about__destination {
    /* Canvas right-aligns this block, so the date sits over the end of
       "Open to relocate" rather than its start. */
    align-items: flex-end;
    text-align: right;
  }
  .about__place-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-family-body);
    font-size: var(--font-size-4xl);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-5xl);
    letter-spacing: -0.4px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-text-emphasis);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Figma text style Manrope/xl/regular-loose — 20px on a 40px line, the same
     pairing overview-section__description uses. */
  .about__from {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-family-body);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-3xl);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
    color: var(--color-text-emphasis);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .about__arrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Takes whatever the two fixed blocks leave — the canvas's 506px is one
       width, not the width. min-width: 0 so a flex item with content can
       actually shrink below its content size. */
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    /* One declaration for both halves: the shaft reads it via currentColor and
       the head's path is fill="currentColor". */
    color: var(--color-icon-default);
    /* Matches the canvas frame's own height, so the row's baseline maths is
       the canvas's. */
    height: 56px;
  }
  .about__arrow-shaft {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    /* 4px is the export's own shaft: its outlined rect spans y 26-30 around
       the line's y=28 centre. Not a guess — measured off the path data. */
    height: 4px;
    background: currentColor;
  }
  .about__arrow-head {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    /* The viewBox's own dimensions, so the head renders at 1:1 and the 26px
       shaft stub lines up with the CSS shaft to the pixel. */
    width: 28.195px;
    height: 29.8464px;
  }

  /* ⚠ This block must stay BELOW the base .about__arrow / .about__destination
     rules. A container query adds no specificity, so `display: none` here and
     `display: flex` there are a straight source-order contest — with this
     block written above them the arrow kept rendering while stacked, which is
     how it shipped for one build of this fix.

     Stacks on its own width, not the viewport's (bug-053).
     The two text blocks are `flex: none` with nowrap labels, so the row has a
     hard floor: 177.6 (place) + 40 + 28.2 (arrowhead) + 40 + 305.7
     (destination) = 591.5px of content that cannot shrink, plus a shaft short
     enough to still read as an arrow — 80px — is 672px, rounded to 680.
     A viewport media query could not see this. At 1024px the sidebar still
     takes 153px and the 3-column grid leaves the body 500.7px, so `≤1023px`
     never fired and the row pushed 22px past the page edge; every width from
     1024 to about 1230 overflowed. Querying the container catches it wherever
     it happens.
     It also *improves* the wide-but-not-desktop case: at a 1023px viewport the
     grid has collapsed to one column and the body is ~943px, which has ample
     room, so the row now keeps its arrow there instead of stacking because a
     breakpoint said so. */
  @container availability (max-width: 680px) {
    .about__availability-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: var(--spacing-lg);
    }
    /* A 506px arrow between two 40px labels needs horizontal room. Rotating it
       to point down would be a different statement, so it goes. */
    .about__arrow {
      display: none;
    }
    .about__destination {
      align-items: flex-start;
      text-align: left;
    }
  }

  /* --- Media placeholders (canvas 2799:3373) --- */

  .about__media {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Canvas: row 2 starts at 1064 against row 1's 1000. */
    gap: 64px;
  }
  .about__media-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 64px;
    /* The tall slot's aspect-ratio sets the row height and the stack fills it
       — the same arrangement the retired portrait row used, and for the same
       reason: something has to decide the row, and a box with no intrinsic
       height cannot. */
    align-items: stretch;
  }
  /* Row 2 mirrors row 1. Reversing the visual order rather than swapping the
     markup keeps both rows identical in the DOM, so the reading order stays
     tall-then-stack and only the paint order flips. */
  .about__media-row--reversed .about__media-tall {
    order: 2;
  }
  .about__media-tall {
    /* Canvas slot is 791.5x1000. */
    aspect-ratio: 791.5 / 1000;
    background: var(--color-bg-muted);
    /* Containing block for the absolutely-positioned media, and `overflow`
       is load-bearing rather than tidiness — see the .about__media-asset
       rule below for why both of these exist. */
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .about__media-stack {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr;
    /* 468 + 64 + 468 = 1000, which is the tall slot exactly. */
    gap: 64px;
  }
  .about__media-slot {
    background: var(--color-bg-muted);
    /* Same pair as .about__media-tall, same reason — see below. */
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* The --color-bg-muted fills above are kept deliberately now that the slots
